<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="ch8-page1-evolutionary-stability" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Evolutionary Stability</title>

  <p>
    In this chapter we study <term>evolutionary stability</term>. Instead of
    two individual players, we think of a large population whose members
    play a game against each other over and over.
  </p>
  <p>
    <alert>Everything in this chapter is for symmetric games.</alert> Recall
    from Chapter 7 that a two-player game is symmetric if both players have
    the same strategies and swapping the players just swaps their payoffs:
    <m>\pi_2(s, t) = \pi_1(t, s)</m>.
  </p>
  <p>
    We will be looking for population states <m>\sigma</m> that are
    <term>evolutionarily stable</term>. One consequence of being
    evolutionarily stable is that <m>(\sigma, \sigma)</m> is a Nash
    equilibrium of the game. We'll see why after the reading.
  </p>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- Reading 1: Section 8.3 up to Theorem 8.1                     -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <assemblage xml:id="read-8-3-part1">
    <title>Section 8.3, Evolutionary Stability (Part 1)</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Section 8.3 up to (but not including) Theorem 8.1.</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      The main ideas to take away:
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            An individual's <term>type</term> <m>\tau</m> is the (possibly
            mixed) strategy that individual uses.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            The <term>state</term> of the population <m>\sigma</m> describes
            how often each pure strategy is used across the whole population.
            Playing against a random member of the population is like
            playing against the mixed strategy <m>\sigma</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            If a small fraction <m>\varepsilon</m> of the population is
            replaced by individuals of type <m>\tau</m>, the new population
            state is <m>(1 - \varepsilon)\sigma + \varepsilon\tau</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    Here is the definition from the reading, restated. A population state
    <m>\sigma</m> is <term>evolutionarily stable</term> if for each
    <m>\tau \neq \sigma</m> there is a number <m>\varepsilon_0 \gt 0</m> such
    that if <m>0 \lt \varepsilon \lt \varepsilon_0</m>, then
    <men xml:id="eq-ch8-ess-def">
      \pi_1\bigl(\sigma, (1 - \varepsilon)\sigma + \varepsilon\tau\bigr)
      \gt
      \pi_1\bigl(\tau, (1 - \varepsilon)\sigma + \varepsilon\tau\bigr).
    </men>
    This is inequality (8.3) in the book.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-epsilon-endpoints">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider the population state
        <m>(1 - \varepsilon)\sigma + \varepsilon\tau</m>. What is it when
        <m>\varepsilon = 0</m>, and what is it when <m>\varepsilon = 1</m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            When <m>\varepsilon = 0</m> it is <m>\sigma</m>; when
            <m>\varepsilon = 1</m> it is <m>\tau</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Right. At <m>\varepsilon = 0</m> we get
            <m>1 \cdot \sigma + 0 \cdot \tau = \sigma</m>, and at
            <m>\varepsilon = 1</m> we get
            <m>0 \cdot \sigma + 1 \cdot \tau = \tau</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            When <m>\varepsilon = 0</m> it is <m>\tau</m>; when
            <m>\varepsilon = 1</m> it is <m>\sigma</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. Plug in: at <m>\varepsilon = 0</m>, the coefficient of
            <m>\sigma</m> is <m>1 - 0 = 1</m> and the coefficient of
            <m>\tau</m> is <m>0</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            It is half <m>\sigma</m> and half <m>\tau</m> in both cases.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. That happens at <m>\varepsilon = \frac{1}{2}</m>. Try
            plugging <m>\varepsilon = 0</m> and <m>\varepsilon = 1</m> into
            the coefficients <m>1 - \varepsilon</m> and <m>\varepsilon</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    So <m>\varepsilon</m> and <m>1 - \varepsilon</m> are weights: as
    <m>\varepsilon</m> goes from 0 to 1, the population shifts from all
    <m>\sigma</m> to all <m>\tau</m>. The definition of evolutionary
    stability only cares about <em>small</em> <m>\varepsilon</m>. Then
    <m>(1 - \varepsilon)\sigma + \varepsilon\tau</m> is almost entirely
    <m>\sigma</m>, with a small fraction of the population using
    <m>\tau</m> instead.
  </p>
  <p>
    Let's look at the inequality again:
    <me>
      \underbrace{\pi_1\bigl(\sigma, (1 - \varepsilon)\sigma + \varepsilon\tau\bigr)}_{\substack{\text{payoff of using } \sigma \text{ against}\\ \text{the invaded population}}}
      \gt
      \underbrace{\pi_1\bigl(\tau, (1 - \varepsilon)\sigma + \varepsilon\tau\bigr)}_{\substack{\text{payoff of using } \tau \text{ against}\\ \text{the invaded population}}}.
    </me>
    So if a small part of the population starts using <m>\tau</m> (because
    of invaders or a mutation), an individual is still better off using
    <m>\sigma</m>. This is why we call <m>\sigma</m> stable: individuals
    using <m>\tau</m> do worse, so <m>\tau</m> should die out, and the
    population returns to all <m>\sigma</m>.
  </p>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- Reading 2: rest of Section 8.3                               -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <assemblage xml:id="read-8-3-part2">
    <title>Section 8.3, Evolutionary Stability (Part 2)</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read the rest of Section 8.3.</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      We will use Theorems 8.1, 8.2, and 8.3 a lot on the homework, so make
      sure you understand what each one says and when it applies.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    For reference, here are the three theorems, restated.
  </p>

  <theorem xml:id="thm-8-1-restated">
    <title>Theorem 8.1</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        A population state <m>\sigma</m> is evolutionarily stable if and only
        if for all <m>\tau \neq \sigma</m>,
        <ol marker="(1)">
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>\pi_1(\sigma, \sigma) \geq \pi_1(\tau, \sigma)</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              If <m>\pi_1(\tau, \sigma) = \pi_1(\sigma, \sigma)</m>, then
              <m>\pi_1(\sigma, \tau) \gt \pi_1(\tau, \tau)</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <theorem xml:id="thm-8-2-restated">
    <title>Theorem 8.2</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        If <m>(\sigma, \sigma)</m> is a strict Nash equilibrium of a symmetric
        two-player game, then <m>\sigma</m> is an evolutionarily stable state
        of the corresponding evolutionary game.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <theorem xml:id="thm-8-3-restated">
    <title>Theorem 8.3</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        If <m>\sigma</m> is an evolutionarily stable state in which all pure
        strategies are active, then for all <m>\tau \neq \sigma</m>,
        <m>(\tau, \tau)</m> is not a Nash equilibrium of the symmetric
        two-player game. Hence there are no other evolutionarily stable
        states.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- Concept checks on Theorems 8.1-8.3                           -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <exercise xml:id="check-ess-implies-nash">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Why is <m>(\sigma, \sigma)</m> always a Nash equilibrium when
        <m>\sigma</m> is an evolutionarily stable state?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Condition (1) of Theorem 8.1 says
            <m>\pi_1(\sigma, \sigma) \geq \pi_1(\tau, \sigma)</m> for every
            <m>\tau</m>, so <m>\sigma</m> is a best response to
            <m>\sigma</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Right. No other strategy <m>\tau</m> does better against
            <m>\sigma</m> than <m>\sigma</m> does. Since the game is
            symmetric, the same holds for Player 2, so
            <m>(\sigma, \sigma)</m> is a Nash equilibrium.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Condition (2) of Theorem 8.1 says
            <m>\pi_1(\sigma, \tau) \gt \pi_1(\tau, \tau)</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. Condition (2) compares how <m>\sigma</m> and
            <m>\tau</m> do against <m>\tau</m>. A Nash equilibrium
            <m>(\sigma, \sigma)</m> is about how strategies do against
            <m>\sigma</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Because the game is symmetric, every symmetric strategy profile
            <m>(\sigma, \sigma)</m> is a Nash equilibrium.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. In the Prisoner's Dilemma, <m>(c, c)</m> is symmetric
            but not a Nash equilibrium.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="check-thm-8-2-from-8-1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Why does Theorem 8.2 follow from Theorem 8.1?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            In a strict Nash equilibrium,
            <m>\pi_1(\sigma, \sigma) \gt \pi_1(\tau, \sigma)</m> for every
            <m>\tau \neq \sigma</m>. So condition (1) holds, and the
            equality in condition (2) never happens, so there is nothing to
            check for condition (2).
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Right. Condition (2) only applies to strategies <m>\tau</m> that
            tie with <m>\sigma</m> against <m>\sigma</m>. In a strict Nash
            equilibrium, there are no ties.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            In a strict Nash equilibrium,
            <m>\pi_1(\sigma, \tau) \gt \pi_1(\tau, \tau)</m> for every
            <m>\tau \neq \sigma</m>, which is condition (2).
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. Being a strict Nash equilibrium is about how strategies
            do against <m>\sigma</m>, not against <m>\tau</m>. Look at what
            happens to the <q>if</q> part of condition (2) instead.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Every Nash equilibrium is strict, so condition (1) is enough.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. Many Nash equilibria are not strict, like the mixed
            Nash equilibrium in Chicken. For those, condition (2) really does
            need to be checked.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    Let's apply Theorem 8.2. Here is the Prisoner's Dilemma again, with the
    Nash equilibrium boxed.
  </p>

  <table xml:id="table-ch8-pd">
    <title>Prisoner's Dilemma</title>
    <tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
      <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
      <col right="minor"/>
      <col/>
      <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
        <cell></cell>
        <cell>Player 2: <m>c</m></cell>
        <cell>Player 2: <m>d</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell>Player 1: <m>c</m></cell>
        <cell><m>(-1, -1)</m></cell>
        <cell><m>(-10, 0)</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>Player 1: <m>d</m></cell>
        <cell><m>(0, -10)</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\boxed{(-6, -6)}</m></cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>

  <exercise xml:id="check-pd-strict-ess">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Notice that <m>(d, d)</m> is a strict Nash equilibrium: if either
        player switches to <m>c</m>, their payoff goes down from
        <m>-6</m> to <m>-10</m>. What can you conclude?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            By Theorem 8.2, the population state <m>d</m> (everyone defects)
            is evolutionarily stable.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Right. A population of defectors can't be invaded by a small
            group of cooperators: the cooperators do worse, so they die out.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            By Theorem 8.2, <m>(d, d)</m> is evolutionarily stable.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Almost! Be careful: evolutionary stability is a property of a
            population state <m>\sigma</m>, not of a strategy profile
            <m>(\sigma, \sigma)</m>. It is the population state <m>d</m> that
            is evolutionarily stable.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Nothing yet. We still need to check condition (2) of Theorem 8.1
            for every <m>\tau</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. That's the point of Theorem 8.2: for a strict Nash
            equilibrium, condition (2) never comes up, so there is nothing
            more to check.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="check-thm-8-3-hypothesis">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider a symmetric game where each player has three pure strategies,
        <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, and <m>c</m>. Suppose the population state
        <m>\sigma = \left(\frac{1}{2}, \frac{1}{2}, 0\right)</m> is
        evolutionarily stable. Can we use Theorem 8.3 to conclude that
        <m>\sigma</m> is the only evolutionarily stable state?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Yes, because <m>\sigma</m> is evolutionarily stable.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. Theorem 8.3 needs more than <m>\sigma</m> being
            evolutionarily stable. Check its other hypothesis.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Yes, because <m>(\sigma, \sigma)</m> is a Nash equilibrium.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. It's true that <m>(\sigma, \sigma)</m> is a Nash
            equilibrium, but that isn't what Theorem 8.3 needs. Check its
            hypotheses.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            No, because strategy <m>c</m> is never used, so not all pure
            strategies are active in <m>\sigma</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Right. Theorem 8.3 only applies when every pure strategy is used
            with positive probability. Here, there could be another
            evolutionarily stable state that uses <m>c</m>. Always check the
            hypotheses before using a theorem!
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="check-thm-8-3-contrapositive">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider a symmetric game where each player has three pure strategies,
        <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, and <m>c</m>. Suppose <m>\sigma</m> uses all three
        pure strategies with positive probability and
        <m>(\sigma, \sigma)</m> is a Nash equilibrium. You also find that
        <m>(a, a)</m> is a Nash equilibrium. What can you conclude about
        <m>\sigma</m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\sigma</m> is not an evolutionarily stable state.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Right. If <m>\sigma</m> were evolutionarily stable, then since
            all pure strategies are active in <m>\sigma</m>, Theorem 8.3
            would say <m>(\tau, \tau)</m> is not a Nash equilibrium for any
            <m>\tau \neq \sigma</m>. But <m>\tau = a</m> gives a Nash
            equilibrium <m>(a, a)</m>, so <m>\sigma</m> can't be
            evolutionarily stable.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\sigma</m> is an evolutionarily stable state, because
            <m>(\sigma, \sigma)</m> is a Nash equilibrium.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. Being a Nash equilibrium is necessary for evolutionary
            stability (condition (1) of Theorem 8.1), but it is not enough.
            Think about what Theorem 8.3 says about <m>(a, a)</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Both <m>\sigma</m> and <m>a</m> are evolutionarily stable states.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. By Theorem 8.3, if <m>\sigma</m> were evolutionarily
            stable, there would be no other evolutionarily stable states, and
            <m>(a, a)</m> couldn't even be a Nash equilibrium.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <interactive xml:id="question-form-8-3" iframe="question-form.html?section=8.3" width="100%" aspect="2:1"/>
</section>
